; case: initialization-order
; verdict: reject
; ambiguous: yes
; tests: externality=pass implementation-independence=fail non-prescription=pass
; failing-test: implementation-independence
; rationale: AMBIGUOUS, resolved REJECT. Ordering constraints sound architectural, and some
;   are — `required-ordering-guarantee` is accepted above. This one is different: it
;   enumerates the concrete boot sequence of selected providers. Test 2 settles it. Choose a
;   different timer provider and the sequence is wrong, so the field describes *this*
;   realization. §5.4 assigns initialization order to joint resolution, and §10.1 derives it
;   into the build plan. What eADL may state is a *dependency* — this service requires that
;   functionality to be available — from which the engine derives an order.
; other-side: belongs in the engine: the initialization dependency graph derived during joint
;   resolution
;
(defsystem app.rt
  (init-order
    (first  clock.pll)
    (then   timer.compare)
    (then   uart.console)
    (finally scheduler.start)))
